@meta
id: 13-12
title: Exactly this many
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-quantifiers
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: sort by the id column in a spreadsheet and hope the widths line up
open: orders.csv

@brief
Two systems are writing into this export. The old one issues four-digit
order ids, `ord_9101`. The new one issues five, `ord_10231`, and
finance's importer chokes on them. Delete every row with a five-digit
id — there are three — and leave the four-digit rows alone.

`/ord_1` is the tempting search and it is wrong: `ord_1024` and
`ord_1832` are old-style ids that happen to start with a one. The
difference is not what the digits are. It is how many there are.

`*`, `\+` and `\?` say "any number", "at least one" and "at most one".
The last quantifier says a number.

@keys
| `\{5}` | exactly five of the atom before it |
| `\{2,4}` | between two and four |
| `\{3,}` | three or more |
| `\{,2}` | up to two |
| `\{-}` | zero or more, but as few as possible — the non-greedy `*` |
| `dd` then `n.` | delete the line, next match, delete that one |

@hints
Say how many digits, not which digits.
--
`\{5}` after an atom means exactly five of it. `ord_\d\{5}` is an
underscore-prefixed id with five digits. Only the opening brace needs
the backslash.
--
`/ord_\d\{5}` `Enter`, `dd`, then `n.` twice.

@solution
/ord_\d\{5}<Enter>  dd  n.  n.  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-two keystrokes. The ids were `10231`, `10244` and `10250`, and
you did not need to know any of them.

`\{n,m}` is the counted quantifier, and it is the only one that lets a
pattern say "this long" rather than "this shape". Fixed-width things
are everywhere in code — `\d\{4}-\d\{2}-\d\{2}` is an ISO date,
`[0-9a-f]\{8}` is a short hash, `\d\{3}` is an HTTP status. Either
bound can be left out: `\{3,}` is three or more, `\{,3}` is up to
three. `\{}` on its own is `*`.

One caveat that catches everyone: `ord_\d\{5}` is a four-digit id
followed by a *fifth* digit, so it matches `ord_10231` but would also
match the first five digits of `ord_102310` if one existed. A count is
a minimum unless you close it off — `ord_\d\{5}\>` says the id stops
there. This file had no six-digit ids, so the short form was enough,
but on data you do not control, add the boundary.

`\{-}` is the odd one out and worth a look. It is `*` with the
opposite appetite: where `.*` takes as much as it can, `.\{-}` takes as
little as it can. `/order=.\{-}request=` stops at the *first* `request=`
after `order=`, not the last. You will not need it often, but when you
do, nothing else works. Other tools spell it `*?`; vim spells it
`\{-}`.

@start orders.csv
order_id,customer_id,currency,amount,status
ord_9101,cus_4471,GBP,1499,PAID
ord_1024,cus_1180,GBP,2599,PAID
ord_9112,cus_4471,GBP,1499,PAID
ord_10231,cus_3302,GBP,4200,PAID
ord_1832,cus_2210,EUR,2599,PAID
ord_9125,cus_7754,GBP,15990,PENDING
ord_10244,cus_1180,GBP,2199,PAID
ord_9131,cus_9021,EUR,1499,PAID
ord_9134,cus_4471,GBP,899,PAID
ord_10250,cus_9021,GBP,1299,PAID
ord_9140,cus_3302,GBP,4200,REFUNDED

@expected orders.csv
